  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A weak area of low pressure will develop along\nthe boundary the stalled frontal zone through today somewhere\nbetween Cape Fear and Oregon Inlet to Cape Lookout. This low\nwill act to lift the front E of its center Nward and consolidate\nthe front W of its center. This combo of cyclo- and \nfrontogenesis, and ample moisture will continue to support \nperiods of rain today, with a few storms over the immediate\ncoastal locales where this location will be in warm sector. \nPrecip to the E of the low's center will be more showery in \nnature, and have allowed NBM's PotThunder to remain in the slgt\nchc range. Instability will be lower today with OVC skies \nthroughout peak heating, so rainfall rates may not get quite as \nhigh and heaviest rain confined to the immediate coast but\nmainly offshore today.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...In the wake of the weekend front, a stiff \nNEerly breeze will develop after midnight MON night and peak \nthrough TUE morning. This will lead to building seas and \nelevated water levels. Astronomical tide levels will luckily be \non the way down, but seas building to 7ft, though relatively \nchoppy periodicity of 7 sec, may be enough to lead to further \nbeach and dune erosion concerns across the areas with severely \ndiminished dune structures. However, this risk appears to be \ndecreasing some due to the strongest winds and highest seas \nshifting south of Hatteras Island. This trend continued to hold \nwith 04OCT 00/06Z model suites. Strongest winds of 20-25 kt with\nhigher gusts expected TUE morning. Winds remain out of the N-NE\ninto late- week, but speeds diminish to 10-15kt late TUE and \nbeyond.",
